	I did enjoy reading the four stories of Borges: "The Garden of Forking
Paths," "The Lottery of Babylon," "The Circular Ruin," and "The Library of Babel."
 The main idea
in each, parallel worlds for each possible outcome, the arbitrariness
of life and a lottery "solution", the creator of life discovering himself to
have been created, and the permutations that produce all possible books are all
interesting ideas yet not new.  The development of each and the particulars of each
are where the strength of the stories seems to lie.
	The idea of the forking of worlds for each possible outcome of an event
is common in cosmology and science fiction.  Cosmology, the science of the evolution
and origin of the universe, often considers this possibility to explain why the
universe is as it, why the physical constants are what they are.  These speculations
lead to considerations of universes with very different physical properties.  In
science fiction, parallel worlds are often used; commonly the story concerns some
interactions between two or more worlds with only slightly different histories.
Borges' story, on the other hand, does not explicitly deal with the different worlds.
They are only alluded to and the plot would stand without the idea, though the story
is meant to be perceived with the idea of forking forefront in the reader's
mind.
	The view of life as a lottery where chance deals out professions, skills, etc.
is old.  The more fair arrangement where the assignments are only temporary, so that
everyone gets many drawings thus tending to equalize is discussed by theoretical
welfare economists.  This story shows an interesting evolution towards this arrangement.
Of course, in this story and many of the others the basic idea applies equally well
as a metaphor for reality.  Life is an infinite lottery.
	"The Circular Ruins" is about dreaming, reality, and dreaming about dreaming
[fi]ad infinitum[fr].  While the main character is concerned with creating a man by
dreams, very little of the story is concerned with the idea or the problems of man
creating man.  Again science fiction writters, philosophers, and others have written
about the possibility that reality is a dream and that a dream can contain a dream
which can contain a dream and so on forever.  Again it was the details, the particular
dreams and thoughts of the main character that fascinated me.
	I found "The Library of Babel" the most interesting of the four stories.
Everyone has heard of the monkeys that, if only they had enough time, would type
all of Shakespeare.  In a combinatorics class one might have the problem of
determining the number of different books in the Library. foot<The answer, by the
way, is ((25^8^0)^4^0)^4^1^0 or a little more than 2 x 10 ^1^,^8^3^4^,^0^9^7 which
is a number containing over one and half million digits which if written in a book
in the Library of Babel would fill up one and quarter books.>  The most interesting
part was the very tempting belief on the part of various sects that the library was
of some use since it contained books explaining all the mysteries of the universe
and books which were indexes to all the other books in the library.  Unfortunately,
any particular book can never be found and so rationally the books are all useless.
What makes the story interesting is that since people (including the reader)
cannot comprehend the gigantic numbers involved, we are tempted by the library.
